WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.330
After lunch, my classroom turns into an

2
00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:06.410
oven, and my pupils start to fall asleep.

3
00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.210
We have a fan, but nobody remembers to

4
00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.190
switch it on until it's too late.

5
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.020
Could the room switch it on by itself?

6
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.940
Today: if-then rules that let

7
00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.440
devices act on their own.

8
00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.970
A rule has two parts: IF a condition

9
00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.410
is true, THEN do an action.

10
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.000
IF the room is above thirty

11
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.260
degrees, THEN switch the fan on.

12
00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:34.410
The microcontroller checks

13
00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.390
every few minutes.

14
00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.570
Rules like this can run all over a school.

15
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.410
IF the tank falls below twenty

16
00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.790
percent, THEN switch the pump on.

17
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.010
IF carbon dioxide passes one thousand

18
00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.310
parts per million, THEN open the

19
00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.430
windows, because at fifteen hundred

20
00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:54.690
the air is already stale.

21
00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.180
The number in a rule is its threshold,

22
00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:00.300
and choosing it takes judgement.

23
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.580
Too low, and the fan runs all

24
00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.140
morning, wasting electricity.

25
00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.010
Too high, and pupils swelter first.

26
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.680
Use your logged data to choose.

27
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:15.210
But one threshold has a problem.

28
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.130
Near thirty degrees the reading

29
00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.330
wobbles: thirty point one,

30
00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.330
twenty-nine point nine, thirty point one.

31
00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:28.030
So the fan goes on, off and on again.

32
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.160
That flicker wears out

33
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:31.360
motors and switches.

34
00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:33.300
Quick check!

35
00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.420
How do we stop the fan flickering on

36
00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.080
and off around thirty degrees?

37
00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:42.310
Use two thresholds: on above thirty

38
00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:45.010
degrees, off only below twenty-eight.

39
00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.320
In between, the fan stays as it is.

40
00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:52.030
A tank pump works the same way: on below

41
00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:54.270
twenty percent, off above ninety.

42
00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.000
What if a sensor fails?

43
00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:00.010
A broken wire can make the tank

44
00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:02.950
sensor read zero, so the pump runs

45
00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.630
and runs, and the tank overflows.

46
00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.510
Fail safe: if a reading is missing or

47
00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:12.230
impossible, stop the pump and tell

48
00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:12.490
someone.

49
00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.830
Before you trust rules, test

50
00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:16.950
them on a day of data.

51
00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:20.490
In the Smart classroom rules lab, a whole

52
00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:23.550
school day replays: minutes too hot,

53
00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:26.670
minutes of stale air, and electricity

54
00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:27.210
used.

55
00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.940
More comfort costs more

56
00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.780
electricity, and someone has to choose.

57
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:34.980
Your turn.

58
00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.820
The tank's level sensor

59
00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:39.580
stops sending readings.

60
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.220
Should the pump stop, or keep

61
00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:45.720
running, just in case?

62
00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:50.130
Stop, and alert someone: when a sensor

63
00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:52.910
fails, the system must fail safe.

64
00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:56.930
Remember: IF a condition THEN an

65
00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.910
action, thresholds from real data, two

66
00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.570
thresholds against flicker, and test

67
00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.370
your rules on a day of data.

68
00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.710
Try it in class tomorrow: rule cards.

69
00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:13.060
Read out a school day, hour by hour, and

70
00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.360
pupils holding the fan, light and pump

71
00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.220
rules stand up whenever their rule says

72
00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:19.800
on.

73
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.880
Then spot the flicker, and fix it.

74
00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.800
Next time: batteries and the sun.
